{# How to read e-mail documents. The message fields come from `doc_email`, the text from
   `read_documents`. Each sentence that names a tool renders only when the run has it. #}
Reading e-mail documents

{% if has('search_collections') %}
Find e-mails with {{ tool('search_collections') }}. Put every form of an address or a name into one call, for example the address, "First Last" and "Last, First".

{% endif %}
{% if has('doc_email') %}
1. Call {{ tool('doc_email') }} with the file_hash of the e-mail. It gives the sender, the recipients, the copies, the date, the subject, the parent message, the attachments and the message graph.
2. To follow a thread, give node, the file hash of another message in the graph. The graph then centres on that message.
3. An attachment is a document of its own. Read it with its own file_hash.
{% endif %}
{% if has('read_documents') %}
4. Read the text of the message with {{ tool('read_documents') }}.
{% endif %}
{% if has('doc_metadata') %}
5. For the headers and the dates that are not in the text, call {{ tool('doc_metadata') }}.
{% endif %}

A copy of the same message can be in more than one mailbox. Compare its fields when a count depends on distinct messages.
